  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n\n  We will use a multi-gradient approach combined with strategic grid resolution. The key idea is to recognize that the optimal h(x) function can be represented as a combination of a few distinct components. We will use a polynomial expansion for the initial guess, then use gradient-based optimization to refine it.\n\n  The rationale is that polynomial functions can capture the necessary structure while being sufficiently flexible. We will start with a low-degree polynomial and allow for higher degrees to capture potential outliers. The bigradient optimization will allow us to escape local optima by checking both forward and backward steps. The grid resolution will be dynamically adjusted based on the performance of local vs. global bisection optimizations.\n\n  We identify several important limitations: the optimization is non-convex and may be sensitive to initialization, and the high-dimensional space requires careful curvature analysis. We are using scientific heuristic optimization rather than rigorous mathematical guarantees.\n\n  This plan is a general optimization strategy that uses advanced gradient-based methods implemented in standard libraries. The essential mathematical components are:\n\n  - Polynomial representation of the function\n  - Bigradient objective function\n  - Dynamic grid resolution\n  - Optimal budget allocation\n  - Modal analysis focusing on the most costly shifts\n\n  Estimations show that this approach can potentially improve on the current 0.3861 bound with sufficient budget.\n\n  1. Mathematical specification:\n\n      We represent the function h as a piecewise polynomial:\n          h(x) = sum_{k=0}^d a_k * x^k   for x in [0,2]\n\n      Truncated to the interval [0,2] and normalized to have integral 1.\n\n      The objective function is:\n          C5 = (2/n) * max_k [ np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') ]\n\n      The variable dimension is d+1.\n\n      The optimization problem is:\n          min a (..polynomial coefficients..) (C5)\n          subject to:\n            - h(x) in [0,1] for all x\n            sum_{x} h(x) dx = 1\n\n      We use a bigradient optimization which computes both forward and backward derivatives to verify improvements, and uses an adaptive learning rate that decays exponentially with iterations but is reset when directional derivatives show no improvement.\n\n  2. Implementation procedure:\n\n      Phase 1: Initialization\n        - Start with parent solution if available (500 points)\n        - If not, generate random polynomial coefficients with varying degrees (2 to 8)\n        - For each polynomial, sample 10-digit random coefficients [0,1] and normalize to have expected integral 1\n\n      Phase 2: Optimization\n        - Build objective function with bigradient calculation:\n          compute_c5_vec(h, n)\n          compute_gradient_step(h, n)\n        - Project feasible function after each step:\n          target = sum(h) * dx = 1\n          adjust h to satisfy target = 1\n        - Use L-BFGS-B with elevation to compute approximate gradients\n        - Apply directional bigradient check on the top 10 shifts to ensure improvement\n\n      Phase 3: Grid resolution:\n        - Dynamic resolution switching: start with n=500\n        - Monitor improvement rate between grid sizes 500 and 1000\n        - If improvement rate drops below 0.0005 (objective improvement per interation), switch to grid of size 1000\n        - Otherwise, reduce resolution to 250\n\n      Phase 4: Local search refinement:\n        - Use a simulated annealing component applied only to shifts showing high correlation (>0.380)\n        - Simulated annealing operates on a small perturbation (either add/remove a small pulse in the function or shift the polynomial coefficients by \u00b11%)\n        - Temperature schedule based on time allocation\n\n  3. Search choices:\n\n      Smooth optimization: use exponentially increasing alpha from its default value\n      Learning rate: adaptive, starts at 0.1 and decays to 0.005 per thousand iterations\n      Polynomial degree: dynamically chosen based on recent performance trends\n      Grid resolution: initially 500 points, to be switched to 1000 if improvement rate (#steps below target) is high.\n\n      Algorithmic variations to try:\n        - Start optimization with random sampling at the same grid\n        - Try polynomial restarts at higher degrees (9-10)\n        - Use a support vector expansion of the polynomial basis\n\n      These variations are tried on a schedule, with the last one tried only if the first four don't show improvement.\n\n  4. Computation budget and robustness:\n\n      Dominant costs: L-BFGS-B iterations on non-convex problem with n~500 and polynomial degree d~8. Total variables ~650.\n\n      Estimated time per L-BFGS iteration: ~0.1 seconds. With max 1000 iterations, ~100s for optimization.\n\n      Budget allocation:\n        - 100s for initial exploration\n        - 200s for gradient-based optimization\n        - 600s for grid adaptation\n        - 200s for local refinement checks\n\n      Time checkpoints at 25%, 50%, 75% of budget.\n\n      Graceful degradation: if exceeds budget, fall back to the best solution found.\n\n  5. Validation and return contract:\n\n      Post-optimization validation:\n        - Re-evaluate C5 with the given correlation function\n        - Check all h[i] in [0,1] and sum(h)*dx==1\n        - If invalid, project and recompute\n\n      Return the best known solution and its bound.",
